   "text": "First, clear the counters by removing all food scraps, utensils, and packaging into the trash or recycling bins. This step comes first because a clean surface is necessary to safely wash dishes and prevents cross-contamination when you start handling clean items.\n\nNext, wipe down the stove and oven exterior with a damp cloth and a little dish soap. This follows the counters because cooking splatters often land on the stove, and cleaning it now prevents grease from drying and becoming harder to remove later.\n\nThen, wash the dishes, pots, and pans in the sink, stacking them in the drying rack as you go. This step comes after the stove is clean because you need a clear, dry area to place the wet items, and washing them now ensures they are ready for storage before you finish the room.\n\nAfter the dishes are handled, wipe down the sink basin and faucet to remove any remaining food particles or soap suds. This comes after the dishes because the sink is the last place you used, and cleaning it now ensures the entire wet zone is spotless before you move to the dry areas.\n\nFinally, sweep or vacuum the floor to pick up any crumbs or spills that may have fallen during cooking or cleaning. This is the last step because it catches any debris that",
